{"name":"Gaia Stone","hex":"#a4be8d","fetched":"2026-10-16","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"stone","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A hard earthen substance that can form rocks; especially, such substance when regarded as a building material.","A piece of such material: a block of stone, rock or a pebble."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Constructed of stone.","Having the appearance of stone."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/stone"}],"entries":[{"title":"Gaia","blocks":[{"text":"In Greek mythology, Gaia, also spelled Gaea, is the personification of Earth. She is the mother of Uranus (Sky), with whom she conceived the Titans, the Cyclopes, and the Giants, as well as of Pontus (Sea), from whose union she bore the primordial sea gods. Her equivalent in the Roman pantheon was Terra."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/36/Gaia_mosaic_antioch.png/330px-Gaia_mosaic_antioch.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gaia"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/36/Gaia_mosaic_antioch.png/500px-Gaia_mosaic_antioch.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/36/Gaia_mosaic_antioch.png/1280px-Gaia_mosaic_antioch.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/36/Gaia_mosaic_antioch.png/500px-Gaia_mosaic_antioch.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AGaia_mosaic_antioch.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d3/Birth_Erikhthonios_Staatliche_Antikensammlungen_2413.jpg/500px-Birth_Erikhthonios_Staatliche_Antikensammlungen_2413.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thu